<article class="env-example" id="062X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/062X">Example <span data-tag="062X">31.19.8</span>.</a> Here is an example of a relative effective Cartier divisor $D$ where the ambient scheme is not flat in a neighbourhood of $D$. Namely, let $A = k[t]$ and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  B = k[t, x, y, x^{-1}y, x^{-2}y, \ldots ]/(ty, tx^{-1}y, tx^{-2}y, \ldots )  \]
</div>
  <p> Then $B$ is not flat over $A$ but $B/xB \cong A$ is flat over $A$. Moreover $x$ is a nonzerodivisor and hence defines a relative effective Cartier divisor in $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B)$ over $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$. </p>
</article>